{
 "encounter_id": "00698",
 "speakers": {
  "person_1": "PATIENT",
  "person_2": "DOCTOR"
 },
 "turns": [
  {
   "s": "person_1",
   "t": "Morning. I'm here about the dizziness. The room spins, mostly when I roll over in bed."
  },
  {
   "s": "person_2",
   "t": "How long has it been going on?"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_1",
   "t": "Since the ear infection. Maybe three weeks after? Or was it before? I can't say when it started exactly."
  },
  {
   "s": "person_2",
   "t": "And how long does each spin last?"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_1",
   "t": "A minute, perhaps. Then it eases. But I feel off for the rest of the day."
  },
  {
   "s": "person_2",
   "t": "Any change in your hearing, or ringing in the ear?"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_1",
   "t": "No. Hearing's the same. No ringing."
  },
  {
   "s": "person_2",
   "t": "What medicines are you taking?"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_1",
   "t": "Betahistine, 16 mg, three times a day, maybe? The GP started it. And amlodipine, 5 mg once a day."
  },
  {
   "s": "person_2",
   "t": "Is the betahistine making a difference, as far as you can tell?"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_1",
   "t": "Not much. Perhaps a little. ~"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_2",
   "t": "Right. Tell me about your health background."
  },
  {
   "s": "person_1",
   "t": "Ear infection, left ear, last spring. High blood pressure. And migraines, for years."
  },
  {
   "s": "person_2",
   "t": "Do you get any aura or headache with the dizziness? ~"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_1",
   "t": "No. Just the spinning. ~"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_2",
   "t": "I'm going to hold off on the balance manoeuvres today, because you're still unsteady, and I'd like the specialist to do them properly. ~"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_1",
   "t": "Fine. ~"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_2",
   "t": "From what you tell me, the spinning when you roll over, lasting a minute, after the ear infection, it fits best with benign positional vertigo."
  },
  {
   "s": "person_1",
   "t": "Benign. That sounds mild. ~"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_2",
   "t": "It's not dangerous. Tiny crystals in the inner ear have moved into the wrong canal, and the brain misreads the signals. ~"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_1",
   "t": "Can it be fixed? ~"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_2",
   "t": "Yes, there's a simple manoeuvre that moves the crystals back, and it often works in one session. ~"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_1",
   "t": "Good. Does it come back? ~"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_2",
   "t": "Sometimes. I'd like to arrange a hearing test as well, to be thorough, given the earlier ear infection."
  },
  {
   "s": "person_1",
   "t": "Fine. ~"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_2",
   "t": "Until then, get up slowly, avoid sudden head turns, and sleep with your head slightly raised."
  },
  {
   "s": "person_1",
   "t": "Right. ~"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_2",
   "t": "And no driving if you feel a spin coming on. ~"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_1",
   "t": "I'll bear it in mind. ~"
  },
  {
   "s": "person_2",
   "t": "I'd like to see you in four weeks, to see how the vertigo is."
  },
  {
   "s": "person_1",
   "t": "Four weeks. Thanks. ~"
  }
 ],
